          <h3>Citing Primary Sources: Search Central Documentation Over SEO Blogs</h3>
          <p>Most veterinary marketing content repeats second-hand SEO advice from generalist blogs. That’s a missed opportunity. When you cite Google’s own Search Central documentation or the Search Quality Rater Guidelines directly, you signal a level of technical sophistication that most local practices lack. It tells the algorithm (and any human evaluator) that your content is grounded in the same standards Google itself publishes.</p>
          <p>This isn’t about dropping links to Google’s help pages. It’s about writing with the confidence of someone who has read the source material. When you explain why E-E-A-T matters for a veterinary page, reference the rater guidelines’ definition of YMYL, not a third-party paraphrase. That depth differentiates your content and aligns it with the exact criteria Google uses to evaluate health information.</p>

          <div class="callout">
            Every medical page on this site is reviewed by a licensed DVM and carries a dated review badge, meeting the heightened trust standards Google applies to health-adjacent content.
          </div>

          <h3>Entity Clarity: Definitional Paragraphs for Conditions and Treatments</h3>
          <p>Search engines don’t read pages the way a pet owner does. They extract entities (the species, the condition, the treatment) and map relationships between them. When your opening paragraph muddles those entities, you make it harder for Google to connect your page to a specific query. Worse, you reduce the chance that an AI Overview will pull your content as a definitive source.</p>
          <p>Start each condition or treatment page with a tight, definitional paragraph. For a page on canine pancreatitis, the first sentence might be: “Canine pancreatitis is an inflammatory condition of the pancreas in dogs that disrupts digestive enzyme production and can become life-threatening without rapid intervention.” That single sentence establishes species, condition, and severity.</p>
          <p>It’s not creative writing. It’s entity clarity, and it directly increases the likelihood of citation in AI-generated answers.</p>

          <h3>Targeting Question-Based Snippets with Concise Answers and FAQ Schema</h3>
          <p>Pet owners type questions into Google: “Is pumpkin safe for cats with constipation?” or “How long after a dog eats chocolate do symptoms appear?” If your page answers that question in a standalone, factual paragraph of 40–60 words, and you wrap that Q&A pair in FAQ structured data, you are competing for the featured snippet and the AI Overview box simultaneously.</p>
          <p>FAQ schema works as a direct signal to Google that a page contains question-answer content suitable for rich results. But the schema is only the container. The answer itself must be concise, definitional, and free of marketing fluff.</p>
          <p>Write the answer as if you were the DVM on call, not the copywriter. Then mark it up. This combination (tight answer plus valid schema) is what pulls a clinic’s content into the zero-click position above the traditional blue links.</p>

          <h3>From the Case Study: Content That Passed Medical Review and Ranked</h3>
          <p>A single condition page tells the story. A clinic’s page on feline lower urinary tract disease sat outside the top 10 for its primary term. The content was accurate but unattributed.</p>
          <p>After a full rewrite under DVM review (adding a dated medical review badge, a definitional opening paragraph that clarified the species and condition, and FAQ schema on three common owner questions), the page climbed into the top 5 within eight weeks. No new backlinks. No technical overhaul. The medical credentials acted as a ranking accelerant, not just a compliance checkbox.</p>
        </div>

