        <!-- Section 11: Maintaining Trust & Accuracy -->
        <div id="maintaining-trust" class="article-anchor">
          <h2>Maintaining Trust and Accuracy Over Time</h2>
          <p>The priority matrix tells you what to fix. This section tells you how to keep it fixed.</p>

          <h3>The Editorial Update Commitment</h3>
          <p>Google’s YMYL classification for veterinary content means your pages are held to a standard that doesn’t age gracefully. A service page last reviewed in 2022 signals neglect, not expertise. The fix is simple and direct: place a visible last updated date at the top of every clinical guide and treatment page. Pair it with a brief editorial note (one sentence) stating that content is reviewed quarterly and revised after major Google core or helpful content updates.</p>
          <p>This practice does more than reassure pet owners. It feeds the E-E-A-T signals quality raters look for on medical sites. When Google’s Helpful Content System evaluates whether your page demonstrates first-hand expertise, a recent DVM review date and a public commitment to accuracy become hard evidence. Without them, you’re asking the algorithm to take your word for it.</p>

          <h3>Quarterly Veterinary SEO Maintenance Calendar</h3>
          <p>Treating SEO as a recurring discipline prevents the slow decay of local and technical signals. A four-month cycle keeps every layer (Map Pack, organic, AI Overviews) in sync without overwhelming a practice manager’s schedule.</p>

          <div class="calendar-list">
            <div class="calendar-item">
              <div class="calendar-month">Month 1</div>
              <div class="calendar-content">
                <strong>Local signals and query expansion.</strong> Audit your GBP for category accuracy, fresh photos, and review velocity. Update the review response protocol to ensure every new review gets a reply within 48 hours. In Search Console, filter for queries ranking on page two and expand those pages with the specific detail searchers expect.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="calendar-item">
              <div class="calendar-month">Month 2</div>
              <div class="calendar-content">
                <strong>Citation consistency and local authority.</strong> Re-check NAP consistency across AVMA’s directory, pet-specific listing sites, and any aggregator feeding Apple Maps. Reach out to one or two local pet businesses or shelters for a relevant backlink: a sponsor page or event mention builds local entity weight.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="calendar-item">
              <div class="calendar-month">Month 3</div>
              <div class="calendar-content">
                <strong>Technical health and mobile experience.</strong> Run a full crawl to catch broken links, redirect chains, and slow-loading widgets. Test every booking and prescription refill widget on a mid-range phone. A single mobile tap delay can tank conversion on a page that ranks well.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="calendar-item">
              <div class="calendar-month">Month 4</div>
              <div class="calendar-content">
                <strong>Clinical content freshness.</strong> Pull every service page that hasn’t been touched in six months. Have your DVM review the medical claims, dosage mentions, and species-specific advice. Even a minor edit resets the freshness signal and reinforces the first-hand expertise that the Helpful Content System rewards.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>We opened with a problem: your clinic is excellent but Google doesn’t know it. Let’s close with the integrated mindset that turns signals into scheduled appointments, starting with your first 30 days.</p>
